2186415012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415040 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc93f710-a95a-5049-bcda-b4e2876fa001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415041 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00e9a03d-cb25-5bd2-bcfe-67d16e1b93d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415041 #edb/ref 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 5\\cdot a & ≡5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{5}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\n.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0db74dcd-d670-5d6c-b7fa-69c8b873b4a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415042 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=10$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,10)=5.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $10$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415043 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ff193af-a6fe-56c6-94b7-27cf32c1ffe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415043 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(6,9)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415044 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f8540b5-4c77-553b-a7f5-1fd3c7ffd53b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415044 #edb/ref 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(3,4)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415045 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $10a≡10b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73a43b1a-0275-523e-ad37-d72b83b2b870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415045 #edb/ref 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10a & ≡10b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(5,3)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415046 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $10a≡10b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n- $9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175