the following statement:\n\n$$\n2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(2,8)=2.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c54d6f9f-c7e1-5ec9-825f-3e37025371fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415048 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=3,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 3a≡3\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3a≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5)\\,⟺\\,3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415049 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca808cf3-f807-5d95-a81f-b5460b52df5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415049 #edb/ref 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415050 #edb/ref 1071 "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=8$\n- $k=-12$\n- $k=-15$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdd37720-ffc4-552d-8b81-f3aa2f866efb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415050 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=8.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=8,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,8)=8\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=8.$\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-12,8)=4\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=-15,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-15,8)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-15.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415051 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d23e3ed0-5575-5880-b9bf-7ff1adfe9936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415051 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(3,12)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3dce9be-488e-597e-98f1-e5e643b35fa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415052 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=2,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 4a≡2\\cdot 4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4a≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415053 #edb/ref 1071 "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=12$\n- $k=3$\n- $k=-5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eaf9f361-8af6-5a67-abf5-86a0bbac7a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415053 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=10.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,10)=2\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=12.$\n\n- If $k=3,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(3,10)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=3.$\n\n- If $k=-5,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-5,10)=5\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
